\f1\fs22 The moment she heard the elevator gulp in Alexander and shut its doors like the mouth of a giant groper, Eloise Shakti pressed a button beneath her desk. The room hummed softly as soundproofing activated, windows automatically tinted, and a small jamming device ensured no electronic eavesdropping could penetrate her office.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With practiced efficiency, she punched a sixteen-digit code into her secure tablet and waited while it connected through seven different encryption protocols and one particularly zealous automated customer service menu.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Guten Tag,\'92 answered a distinctly Swiss-German voice after the fourth ring.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Activate the VorpalTech contingency,\'92 Eloise said without preamble.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A pause. \'91Is that a new Robert Ludlum novel?\'92 What followed was culturally attuned people might call a German belly-laugh but which common folk would hear as an admonition like, \'91Your toaster is two degrees off square on your kitchen bench.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise pinched the bridge of her nose. \'91No, Heinrich, it's not a novel. It's the emergency protocol we established for precisely this situation.\'91\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Ah, yes. The, er\'97\'92 Papers shuffled in the background. \'91The Supervising Executive Director of Pan-European Audiovisual Communications and Strategic Insights position?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That's the one.\'91\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I take it\'97\'92 Heinrich was about to name Alexander and realised the folly of it. \'91I take the candidate has already accepted the assignment?\'91\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91He's on his way to the airport as we speak.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The voice on the other end of the line made a sound like a man who has just realised his parachute was packed by an intern with a grudge. \'91
\f2\i Mein Gott
\f1\i0 ! He's flying over directly? 
\f2\i Heute?
\f1\i0 \'91\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, Heinrich. 
\f2\i Heute
\f1\i0 . Today.\'92 Eloise\'92s voice dropped ten degrees. \'91Is that a problem?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91With all due respect, Frau Shakti, we expected at least a week's notice. VorpalTech AG exists only on paper and as a post office box in Vaduz. We have no actual facility, no staff, and certainly no\'97what was it? Let me check the  brief. No \'85 quantum reality lenses.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, of course you don\'92t have them. You don\'92t have hover boards either.\'92 Eloise examined her manicure with the casual menace of someone who could, with one phone call, ensure Heinrich's next job involved taste-testing experimental protein supplements in Antarctica. \'91The job is to make the candidate believe they exist, not actually produce them. Who do you think I think you are? Oppenheimer?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I would rather you not mention this person,\'92 Heinrich said, still chafing over the fact the Americans got the bomb \'91Even so, you want a fully operational technology company? In Liechtenstein? By tomorrow? This is not in the contract.\'92 Heinrich's voice climbed several octaves. \'91It's simply not possible!\'91\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I pay you obscene amounts of money precisely because you make the impossible happen on short notice,\'91 Eloise replied.\
